<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sahrawi Republic, SADR</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic is a partially recognised state in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> that claims <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a> but controls only its easternmost portion. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polisario-front">Polisario Front</a> proclaimed it on 27 February 1976 at Bir Lehlou, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> Accords in November 1975 to divide the territory without any role for the Sahrawi people. Morocco occupies the rest and calls it its Southern Provinces. The republic is a full member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a>, and has been recognised by some states, of which a number have since frozen or withdrawn recognition. Its government operates largely from refugee camps near Tindouf in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name Sahrawi is the romanisation of the Arabic word Ṣaḥrāwī, meaning inhabitant of the desert, from ṣaḥrāʼ, desert. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a>, inhabited predominantly by nomadic Sahrawi people, had been a Spanish possession since the late 19th century. The republic's claimed capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laayoune">Laayoune</a>, the largest city of Western Sahara, which it does not control. It has a temporary capital at Tifariti, formally established on 27 February 2008, while most day-to-day administration takes place in Rabuni, one of the Sahrawi refugee camps in Tindouf Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polisario-front">Polisario Front</a> was formally constituted in 1973 and at once launched an armed struggle against Spanish rule. With pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a>, this led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to announce an independence referendum. In 1975, however, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-march">Green March</a>, a political demonstration coordinated by the Moroccan government, Spain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> signed the Madrid Accords on 14 November 1975, dividing the territory between Morocco and Mauritania with no role for the Polisario Front or the Sahrawi people. Spain withdrew its forces by 12 January 1976, and Moroccan and Mauritanian forces occupied the territory. Neither state gained international recognition, and war followed with the Polisario Front. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> considers the Polisario Front the legitimate representative of the Sahrawi people.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polisario-front">Polisario Front</a> proclaimed the republic on 27 February 1976, saying that a new entity was needed to fill the political void left by the departing Spanish administration. A ceasefire followed in 1991. The Baker Plan, endorsed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 2003, would have replaced the republic with a five-year Western Sahara Authority before a referendum, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> declined to take part. In April 2007 Morocco proposed an autonomous self-governing entity, and the United Nations asked the parties to negotiate directly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The republic governs the part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a> east of the Moroccan wall, as well as the refugee camps near Tindouf, and claims all of the territory; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> calls the rest its Southern Provinces. Modern Standard Arabic is the constitutionally recognised official language, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassaniya-arabic">Hassaniya Arabic</a> is the common vernacular, and Spanish is widely used as a second language. Maliki <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> is the official religion. The republic has been a full member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a> since 1982, and it is not a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It belongs to the Arab League, the Arab Maghreb Union and the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation; it left the Organisation of African Unity in 1984 when the <b>Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic</b> was admitted and rejoined the African Union in 2017.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
